faq:
  - q: "Is it OK to negotiate salary by email?"
    a: "Yes — email is a legitimate and sometimes preferable negotiation channel. It gives you time to choose your words carefully, creates a written record, and removes the pressure of real-time conversation. Some recruiters prefer email for offer discussions. If given a choice, phone or video allows more nuance and real-time back-and-forth — but email is effective and professional."
  - q: "How do you write a salary negotiation email?"
    a: "Four components: (1) Open with genuine appreciation and enthusiasm. (2) State your counter clearly — a specific number, not a range. (3) Anchor to market data and role scope — not personal need or expenses. (4) Keep the door open — express flexibility and eagerness to make it work. Keep the email under 200 words. Shorter is almost always better."
  - q: "Should you give a range or a specific number when negotiating?"
    a: "A specific number. Giving a range anchors the negotiation at the bottom — the employer will almost always respond to the lower end. 'I was hoping to be at $185,000' is stronger than 'I was thinking somewhere between $175,000 and $190,000.' State your number. Be specific."
  - q: "How long should a salary negotiation email be?"
    a: "Under 200 words. Shorter emails are read. Long emails get skimmed. Every additional sentence dilutes the core message. State your appreciation, state your number, anchor it briefly, and close with eagerness to proceed."
  - q: "What is the best subject line for a salary negotiation email?"
    a: "Keep it neutral and professional. 'Re: [Role Name] Offer' or 'Following up on my offer — [Your Name]' are both appropriate. Do not use subject lines that telegraph the negotiation ('Counter offer for [Role]') — this can create unnecessary friction before the email is even read."
